<p>Today's brief covers stories from 30 October to 2 November that earlier briefs did not include.</p>

<figure class="dgm">
<div class="dgm-stack">
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Business use</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span class="hl">ADNOC<em>agents across operations</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Model providers</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span class="hl">Anthropic<em>compute commitments disclosed</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Edge and web apps</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span>Ivanti Connect Secure<em>VPN gateway flaws</em></span><span>Drupal Commerce<em>store access bypass</em></span></div></div>
</div>
<figcaption>AI spend and adoption at the top; the same old patching discipline at the edge.</figcaption>
</figure>

<section class="block">
<h2>1. Anthropic's amended S-1 shows the scale of AI compute commitments</h2>
<p>Anthropic filed an amended IPO registration on 2 November adding third-quarter results, with revenue of about $17 billion for the quarter, and disclosed about $895.4 billion of purchase commitments and lease agreements<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>. Coverage reports nine-month revenue of $33.2 billion and says substantially all of the commitments relate to compute and infrastructure<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>.</p>
<p><strong>CTO take:</strong> the model providers you build on are making very long, very large bets on compute. That is good for capacity, but it also means pricing, contracts and capacity allocation will be shaped by their need to fill those commitments. Keep your stack portable across providers and know your cost per task; see <a href="/insights/ai-capacity-crunch-cloud-planning/">planning around the AI capacity crunch</a> and <a href="/insights/llm-model-routing/">model routing</a>.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>2. ADNOC signs up to run AI agents with OpenAI and G42</h2>
<p>ADNOC announced a multi-year agreement with OpenAI and G42 on 2 November to build and run AI agents on OpenAI's Frontier platform across its operations, including forecasting crude supply, demand, prices and margins<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup>. The systems will run on G42's UAE-based infrastructure, with human experts keeping oversight of consequential decisions; financial terms were not disclosed<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-4">4</a></sup>.</p>
<p><strong>CTO take:</strong> agent platforms are moving from pilots into core operations at large industrial firms, with in-country hosting and human sign-off built into the deal. If you sell into Gulf enterprises, expect buyers to ask for exactly that combination. See <a href="/insights/agents-replace-seats-enterprise-saas/">when agents become the user</a> and the <a href="/uae/">UAE page</a>.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>3. CISA adds two more Ivanti Connect Secure flaws to its exploited list</h2>
<p>CISA added two Ivanti Connect Secure vulnerabilities to its Known Exploited Vulnerabilities catalogue on 30 October, with federal patch deadlines in early and mid-November<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-5">5</a></sup>. The Hacker News reports attackers chaining Connect Secure flaws to gain unauthenticated root access<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-6">6</a></sup>.</p>
<p><strong>CTO take:</strong> VPN gateways remain the front door attackers try first. Patch now, check for signs of compromise rather than assuming the patch is enough, and keep the management interface off the internet. The pattern is covered in <a href="/case-studies/us-utility-management-plane-hardening/">hardening a utility's management plane</a>.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>4. A highly critical Drupal Commerce flaw is being exploited</h2>
<p>Drupal says an access bypass in the Commerce Core module, CVE-2026-118539, is being actively exploited, and asks store owners to update and audit their orders for inconsistencies<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-7">7</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-8">8</a></sup>. Commerce Core is a contributed module, not Drupal core itself.</p>
<p><strong>CTO take:</strong> if you run Drupal Commerce, update to the fixed release named in Drupal's advisory today and review recent orders and payments. For older stores, this is a good moment to inventory which contributed modules handle money and who maintains them.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>What I'd do this week</h2>
<ul>
<li>Patch Ivanti Connect Secure and Drupal Commerce if you run them, then check for compromise.</li>
<li>Review your AI provider contracts for pricing and capacity terms that could change.</li>
<li>If you sell into Gulf enterprises, prepare an answer on in-country hosting and human oversight for agents.</li>
</ul>
<p>See <a href="/regions/">where I work</a>, or <a href="/#assess">analyse your challenge</a> and bring the result to a call.</p>
</section>
